@startuml "Strategy - Pricing (Solution) - Class Diagram"
skinparam classAttributeIconSize 0
hide empty members
title After — the campaign is an object the till holds

class Checkout <<Context>> {
  - rule : PricingRule
  + ring(basket) : Receipt
  + setRule(PricingRule)
  + ruleName() : String
}

interface PricingRule <<Strategy>> {
  + name() : String
  + priceFor(basket) : Money
}

class ShelfPrice <<ConcreteStrategy>>
class PercentageOff <<ConcreteStrategy>> {
  - percent : int
  - qualifies : Predicate<Basket>
}
class TieredPercentageOff <<ConcreteStrategy>> {
  - threshold : Money
}
class CheapestOfEveryThird <<ConcreteStrategy>> {
  - category : String
}

class CampaignBook {
  - rules : List<PricingRule>
  + add(PricingRule) : CampaignBook
  + bestFor(basket) : PricingRule
  + quoteAll(basket) : List<Receipt>
}

Checkout o--> "rule" PricingRule
PricingRule <|.. ShelfPrice
PricingRule <|.. PercentageOff
PricingRule <|.. TieredPercentageOff
PricingRule <|.. CheapestOfEveryThird
CampaignBook o--> "*" PricingRule

note right of PricingRule
  <b>Two methods, both about the algorithm.</b>
  No appliesTo, no priority, no isBetterThan —
  the moment a rule ranks itself against the
  others, every rule knows every other rule.
end note

note bottom of CheapestOfEveryThird
  Not a percentage at all. Had the interface
  been <b>int percentOff()</b>, this campaign
  could not have been written.
end note

note bottom of CampaignBook
  <b>Who chooses</b> (GoF implementation issue 1):
  not the strategies, not the context. A small
  object whose only job is choosing.
end note

note left of Checkout
  <b>The rule is a field.</b> One till prices one
  basket five ways, and names no campaign class
  to do it.
end note

@enduml
